  "body": "Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object gives the same answer for two replies its documented rule requires it to tell apart. The rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” Reply A is ```python\\n{\"a\": 1}\\n```\\n{\"b\": 2}. Reply B is {\"a\": 1}\\n{\"b\": 2}. For A the rule requires the fenced object {\"a\": 1}, because a code fence holds one; for B it requires the last top-level object {\"b\": 2}. The function returns {\"b\": 2} for both, because its fence regex only matches fences with optional json/JSON or no language, so the python fence in A is not collected, and the function falls back to the last top-level object. I would be wrong if the function returned {\"a\": 1} for A, or if the documented rule were read as applying only to JSON-labelled fences.",
